.TH MKSTEMP 3
.SH NAME
mkstemp \- make a unique file and open it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int mkstemp(char *template);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mkstemp
replaces
.I template
by a unique file name, creates that file with mode 0600, and returns a
descriptor open on it for reading and writing.  It returns \-1 if no name can be
found or the file cannot be made.  The template should look like a file name with
six trailing X's, which are replaced with the current process id and a unique
letter, exactly as
.IR mktemp (3)
does; the template must be
.B writable
\- a string literal will not do, this machine's literals living in the read\-only
const segment.
.PP
It is POSIX's, not v7's and not C11's.
.I Mktemp
hands back a name and leaves the caller to open it;
.I mkstemp
opens it itself, which is the whole of the difference.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mktemp(3), creat(2), open(2), fopen(3) for
.I tmpfile
and
.I tmpnam
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/mkstemp.c
.SH BUGS
.B The creation is not atomic on this machine.
POSIX obtains its guarantee from
.BR O_CREAT | O_EXCL ,
and this kernel has neither
.RI ( <fcntl.h> ):
creation is reachable only through
.IR creat (2),
which does not fail on a file that already exists.  So
.I mkstemp
tests the name with
.IR access (2)
and creates it immediately afterwards, which is
.IR mktemp (3)'s
own race and not an improvement on it.  What it does buy over
.I mktemp
is that the file exists, and is open read\-write, before the caller writes a
byte \- so nothing downstream has to open a name a second time.
.PP
.B It walks a single letter,
inheriting
.IR mktemp (3)'s
reach: 27 names for a given process and no more.
